## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design scalable multi-tenant data isolation using AWS IAM, Lake Formation, and S3 policies across multiple regions. It explores understanding of cross-region replication, governance controls, and auditability for SaaS applications.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped IAM roles with STS AssumeRole session tags
- Lake Formation permissions and data lake zone isolation
- S3 replication, encryption (SSE-KMS), and access boundaries
- Least-privilege access enforcement through bucket policies
- Auditing with CloudTrail, AWS Config, and Access Analyzer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy snippet for tenant role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::${tenant_account_id}:role/TenantUser"},
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
    "Condition": {
      "StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${tenant_id}"},
      "StringLike": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${tenant_id}"}
    }
  }]
}

// S3 bucket policy for tenant isolation
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Deny",
    "Principal": "*",
    "Action": "s3:*",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::datalake-bucket/*",
    "Condition": {
      "StringNotEquals": {"s3:prefix": ["${tenant_id}/*"]},
      "Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}
    }
  }]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-account IAM and S3 permissions, a common real-world pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account roles and trust policies
- Bucket policies and principal scoping
- Least privilege and auditability
- Testing: assume role and perform PutObject

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy for cross-account role in Account B
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/LambdaExecutionRole" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Inline policy for cross-account role allowing PutObject
{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Allow",
      "Action":["s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b-name/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Bucket policy in Account B
{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[
    {
      "Effect":"Allow",
      "Principal":{"AWS":"arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/LambdaExecutionRole"},
      "Action":"s3:PutObject",
      "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-b-name/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify if the Lambda runs in a VPC without internet access?
- How would you enforce per-object ACLs or S3 object tagging for billing separation?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses advanced AWS ABAC, cross-account governance, and policy-as-code in a multi-tenant data lake; targets practical use of IAM Identity Center, PrincipalTags, Lake Formation, and a GitOps deployment flow.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC with session/principal tags
- Lake Formation fine-grained permissions
- Cross-account policy deployment (GitOps)
- Revocation latency and audits (CloudTrail, Config, Access Analyzer)

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:saml-provider/AWS_SSO" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "tenant-A"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-A/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": { "s3:prefix": "tenant-A/" }
      }
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["lakeformation:GetDataLocation", "lakeformation:GetTable", "lakeformation:GrantPermissions", "glue:GetDatabase", "glue:GetTable"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": { "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "tenant-A" }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test revocation latency end-to-end? 
- How would you scale this across many tenants and regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement real-time, policy-driven containment in a multi-tenant data lake, focusing on automated incident response, policy-as-code, and auditability.

## Key Concepts

- Lake Formation permissions and ABAC via IAM tags
- CloudTrail + EventBridge for event-driven enforcement
- Data quarantine patterns and catalog synchronization
- GitOps workflows for policy deployment and rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-Lambda: react to leakage event
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  // parse tenant and offending principal
  const tenant = event.detail.tenant;
  const principal = event.detail.principal;
  // revoke Lake Formation permissions for offending principal on tenant data
  await revokeLFPermissions(tenant, principal);
  // quarantine data and update catalog
  await quarantineTenantData(tenant);
  // push policy changes to GitOps repo and notify
  await pushPolicyGitOps(tenant);
  return { status: 'ok' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test latency and ensure no data leakage during rollback?
- How would you handle false positives and maintain tenant trust while preserving audit trails?
